{"gid":"2026_01_NE_SEA","week":1,"away":"NE","home":"SEA","away_col":"#7FA8C9","home_col":"#69BE28","spread":3.5,"total":44.5,"fav":"SEA","hpts":24,"apts":20,"kickoff":"Wed Sep 9 \u00b7 20:20","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Hunter Henry</strong> has room to work \u2014 SEA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p>The <strong>SEA</strong> host the <strong>NE</strong> as favorites of <strong>3.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>44.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>SEA 24\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>33% of its snaps with SEA in front</strong>, <strong>42% within one score</strong>, and <strong>25% with SEA chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to SEA</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while NE leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong> \u2014 SEA are stingy against the run (106 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Drake Maye</strong> adds rushing value \u2014 he scrambles on 5.8% of dropbacks, live for rush-yard and anytime-TD props.</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 NE hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><p><strong>A.J. Brown</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 SEA hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to SEA \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Jadarian Price</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p>A middling 44 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>SEA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>SEA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jadarian Price</strong> (16 carries, 68 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though SEA's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> projects for <strong>13 targets and 113 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>AJ Barner</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 37 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NE \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>NE are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>39%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NE project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong>'s rushing projection (22 carries, 93 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>A.J. Brown</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NE funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 94 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Hunter Henry</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 49 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"NE","col":"#7FA8C9","players":[{"name":"Drake Maye","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":236.66,"book":null,"over_th":222.3,"under_th":245.92,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.65,"book":null,"over_th":1.04,"under_th":1.42,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":18.9,"fair":"9/2","book":null,"value":null},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":236.66,"q":[104.68,176.24,233.81,294.36,362.85],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"pass_td":{"m":1.65,"q":[0.26,0.26,1.23,2.2,3.17],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_yards":{"m":33.11,"q":[0.0,11.41,27.78,48.83,72.21],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_att